---
title: Capabilities
order: 4
description: Linux capabilities — getcap, setcap
tags: [permissions, capabilities, getcap, setcap, cap]
---

Linux capabilities split root's monolithic privileges into distinct units. Instead of granting full root via SUID, grant only the specific privilege a program needs.

@text
Capability sets:

  Effective (e)   — capabilities currently active for the process
  Permitted (p)   — capabilities the process may use (can be enabled into effective)
  Inheritable (i) — capabilities preserved across execve()

  File capability notation: cap_name+eip
    e = effective (raise on exec)
    i = inheritable
    p = permitted

@text
View capabilities with getcap.

@code bash
# Show capabilities on a file
getcap /usr/bin/ping

# Example output:
# /usr/bin/ping cap_net_raw=ep

# Recursively find all files with capabilities
sudo getcap -r / 2>/dev/null

# Check specific directories
getcap -r /usr/bin/ 2>/dev/null
getcap -r /usr/sbin/ 2>/dev/null

# Show capabilities of a running process
cat /proc/$(pgrep -x program)/status | grep -i cap

# Decode capability hex values
capsh --decode=00000000a80425fb

@text
Set capabilities with setcap.

@code bash
# Allow a program to bind to privileged ports (< 1024) without root
sudo setcap 'cap_net_bind_service=+ep' /usr/bin/myserver

# Allow raw network access (e.g., ping without SUID)
sudo setcap 'cap_net_raw=+ep' /usr/bin/myping

# Allow reading any file (like root read access)
sudo setcap 'cap_dac_read_search=+ep' /usr/bin/mytool

# Set multiple capabilities
sudo setcap 'cap_net_bind_service,cap_net_raw=+ep' /usr/bin/myserver

# Remove all capabilities from a file
sudo setcap -r /usr/bin/myserver

@text
Commonly used capabilities:

  cap_chown              — change file ownership
  cap_dac_override       — bypass file read/write/execute permission checks
  cap_dac_read_search    — bypass file read and directory search checks
  cap_fowner             — bypass permission checks on file owner operations
  cap_kill               — send signals to any process
  cap_net_admin          — network administration (configure interfaces, firewall)
  cap_net_bind_service   — bind to ports below 1024
  cap_net_raw            — use raw and packet sockets (ping, tcpdump)
  cap_setuid             — change UID
  cap_setgid             — change GID
  cap_sys_admin          — broad sysadmin (mount, swapon, sethostname, many more)
  cap_sys_chroot         — use chroot
  cap_sys_ptrace         — trace/debug any process
  cap_sys_time           — set system clock

@text
Practical examples.

@code bash
# Allow a web server to bind port 80/443 without root
sudo setcap 'cap_net_bind_service=+ep' /usr/bin/caddy

# Allow tcpdump to capture without root
sudo setcap 'cap_net_raw,cap_net_admin=+ep' /usr/bin/tcpdump

# Allow a backup tool to read all files
sudo setcap 'cap_dac_read_search=+ep' /usr/local/bin/backup

# Verify after setting
getcap /usr/bin/caddy

@text
Capabilities vs SUID — prefer capabilities when possible.

@code bash
# Instead of SUID root:
sudo chmod u+s /usr/bin/myprogram        # gives ALL root powers

# Use capabilities for least privilege:
sudo setcap 'cap_net_raw=+ep' /usr/bin/myprogram  # only raw sockets

# Check what a program actually needs with strace
sudo strace -f -e trace=process /usr/bin/program 2>&1 | grep -i cap

# List all capabilities
capsh --print

@text
Security notes:
- Capabilities on scripts are ignored (same as SUID) — only binaries
- Files with capabilities must not be writable by unprivileged users
- cap_sys_admin is nearly equivalent to full root — avoid it
- Capabilities survive copy but not move across filesystems
- Package updates may reset capabilities — use a post-install hook
